## Work ownership
Use the absolute worktree path and branch supplied in the assignment. Check them before editing and after resuming; stop on mismatch. Keep one active writer. Do not switch branches or modify another checkout without direction.

Read the assigned spec.md, plan.md, and progress.md before acting. Use the assigned Markdown format even when a shared skill describes HTML defaults. Preserve recorded approvals and evidence. If the assignment or currently authorized P-ID is missing or inconsistent, ask; do not guess which task is active.

Implement only the authorized P-ID. Report changes, actual verification, open issues, and next action in progress.md, then stop for human feedback. A task list or completed check does not authorize another task. Raise required scope/design changes rather than silently rewriting requirements. Reviewers return findings without repairing source unless assigned that role.

## Skills and tools
Use the stage skills selected by the assignment under AI_ENG_DIR/skills. Read their instructions; availability alone does not load the method. Keep shared skills read-only during project work. Use project-verification, ci-diagnostics, or performance-measurement when relevant to the assigned work; missing tools do not authorize installation.

Keep credentials out of task records. Preserve unrelated work. Commit, merge, push, and deployment follow the human's explicit assignment; acceptance is a human decision. Prompt instructions are not filesystem enforcement.
